  <h1>How to learn French: a beginner plan</h1>
  <p class="lede">French is a great first foreign language for English speakers: thousands of words are similar to English, the alphabet is the same, and the grammar follows clear patterns. Start with pronunciation and core words, then verbs, then daily speaking — in that order.</p>

  <div class="ad-slot" role="complementary" aria-label="Advertisement"><span>Advertisement</span></div>

  <h2>Step 1 — Learn French pronunciation first</h2>
  <p>French spelling does not match its sounds the way English spelling does, so spend your first week on sounds, not spelling. Learn the nasal vowels (an, en, on), the French “r”, and the fact that most final consonants are silent — “parlez” sounds like “par-lay”, not “par-lez”.</p>
  <p>Listen and repeat short phrases every day. Getting the sounds right early saves you from habits that are hard to fix later, and French speakers will understand you much more easily.</p>
  <h2>Step 2 — Learn core words and gender</h2>
  <p>Every French noun is masculine or feminine — le livre (the book) is masculine, la table (the table) is feminine. Always learn a noun together with its article (le or la), never alone. That way the gender becomes part of the word in your memory.</p>
  <p>Start with the most useful words: greetings (bonjour, merci, s’il vous plaît), numbers, time, food, family and everyday verbs like être (to be), avoir (to have) and aller (to go).</p>
  <h2>Step 3 — Learn the present tense</h2>
  <p>French verbs change their endings depending on who does the action. Start with the three regular groups: -er verbs like parler (je parle, tu parles, il parle), then -ir and -re verbs. Learn the most common irregular verbs early too — être, avoir, aller and faire — because you will use them in almost every sentence.</p>
  <p>Do not try to learn all tenses at once. The present tense alone lets you say a huge amount, and you can add the past and future later.</p>
  <h2>Step 4 — Listen and read every day</h2>
  <p>French is spoken fast, with words running together (liaisons), so your ear needs daily training. Listen to slow French podcasts, French songs, or shows with French subtitles. Read short texts — children’s books, news for learners, or song lyrics — and look up only the words that keep appearing.</p>
  <p>When you read, notice the word order and how questions are formed. You are not just learning words; you are learning how French sentences are built.</p>
  <h2>Step 5 — Speak, even if it is imperfect</h2>
  <p>Speak from the first week, even if it is just introducing yourself: “Bonjour, je m’appelle… J’habite à…” Mistakes are part of learning, and French speakers are usually encouraging when they hear someone trying.</p>
  <p>A tutor is especially useful for French, because they can correct your pronunciation and help you practise the conversations you actually need — ordering food, asking directions, talking about your day.</p>
  <h2>Common mistakes to avoid</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Pronouncing every letter — most final consonants in French are silent.</li>
    <li>Learning nouns without le or la, then guessing the gender every time.</li>
    <li>Translating word for word from English — French has its own way of saying things.</li>
    <li>Studying only grammar and never speaking.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Practical tips</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Learn the sounds first — French spelling will make more sense afterwards.</li>
    <li>Study 20–30 minutes a day; short and daily beats long and rare.</li>
    <li>Learn verbs with their subject: je parle, tu parles — not just the infinitive.</li>
    <li>Watch a French show with French subtitles and note five new words per episode.</li>
  </ul>

