  <h2>Questions people ask</h2>

  <details><summary>How long does it take to learn French?</summary><p>French is one of the quicker languages for English speakers. With 30 minutes a day, most learners can handle simple conversations in 3–6 months and reach a comfortable everyday level in 1–2 years. The shared vocabulary with English gives you a big head start.</p></details>
  <details><summary>Is French hard to learn?</summary><p>French has tricky parts — pronunciation, noun gender and verb endings — but the alphabet is familiar, thousands of words look like English words, and the grammar is regular once you learn the patterns. Most learners find French very achievable.</p></details>
  <details><summary>Do I need to learn masculine and feminine nouns?</summary><p>Yes, and it is easier than it sounds. Always learn the article with the noun — le chat, la maison — and the gender becomes automatic over time. Guessing later is much harder than learning it with the word from the start.</p></details>
  <details><summary>Can I learn French by myself?</summary><p>Yes, for vocabulary, grammar and reading. But speaking and pronunciation need feedback from a real person, which is where a tutor helps most.</p></details>
